WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:02.220
Welcome to BBC Learning English.

00:00:02.220 --> 00:00:05.600
I'm Sam and today I'm happy.

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:08.640
In fact, I'm very happy.

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:10.200
I'm really happy.

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:11.260
Thrilled!

00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:12.840
I'm really thrilled!

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:15.560
I'm absolutely thrilled!

00:00:15.560 --> 00:00:19.260
My happiness keeps getting bigger and bigger.

00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:22.440
I started with the word 'happy'.

00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:28.060
'Happy' is a gradable adjective, so I can be happy – here.

00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:30.520
Quite happy – here.

00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:33.470
Very or really happy – here.

00:00:33.470 --> 00:00:37.600
What's another way to say 'very' or 'really happy'?

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:38.990
Thrilled!

00:00:38.990 --> 00:00:41.740
'Thrilled' is not a gradable adjective.

00:00:41.740 --> 00:00:44.200
It's an extreme adjective.

00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:45.930
It's already up here.

00:00:45.930 --> 00:00:48.080
So, can we make it stronger?

00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:48.940
Yes!

00:00:48.940 --> 00:00:52.120
You can use 'really'. I'm really thrilled.

00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:55.520
Or 'absolutely'. I'm absolutely thrilled.

00:00:55.520 --> 00:00:57.840
But you can't use 'very'.

